\begin_layout Section
Impurity-only enrichment for the ground state
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The ground-state computation can be viewed as being close in spirit to the
 time-evolution computation after the formal replacement
\begin_inset Formula $dt\mapsto -i\,d\tau$
\end_inset

.
 The useful analogy is not exact, but it is a good guide for how to make
 the active-space enrichment cheaper.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the present spinless ground-state code, enrichment connects the whole
 active window to the Slater sector.
 In code this corresponds to selecting representative Slater orbitals from
\begin_inset Formula $K_{AS}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is the current active window and 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 is the Slater sector.
 Schematically,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
K=\begin{pmatrix}
K_{II} & K_{IB} & K_{IS}\\
K_{BI} & K_{BB} & K_{BS}\\
K_{SI} & K_{SB} & K_{SS}
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
A=I\cup B .
\]

\end_inset

The current GS enrichment uses information from 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{AS}=(K_{IS},K_{BS})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the dynamics code, the interaction picture removes the bath Hamiltonian
 from the explicit TDVP problem.
 Therefore representative orbitals are selected only through the impurity-bath
 coupling,
\begin_inset Formula $K_{IS}$
\end_inset

.
 This suggests a cheaper GS heuristic: before enrichment, rotate the Slater
 bath to a basis where the Slater bath block is diagonal, then enrich using
 impurity rows only.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Important difference from real time
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For real time, if
\begin_inset Formula $K_{SS}=\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_k)$
\end_inset

, bath-only evolution is a phase:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
b_k(t+dt)=e^{-i\epsilon_k dt}b_k(t).
\]

\end_inset

For imaginary time / ground-state evolution, it is instead an exponential
 weight:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
b_k(\tau+d\tau)=e^{-\epsilon_k d\tau}b_k(\tau).
\]

\end_inset

So it is not literally a phase in the GS problem.
 But for enrichment we do not need an exact imaginary-time evolution of the
 Slater sector.
 We only need a cheap way to identify useful Slater directions to promote
 into the active window.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Proposed cheap enrichment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The minimal version is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Rotate the Slater block so that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
K_{SS}=\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_k).
\]

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Build the representative-orbital SVD only from impurity rows,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
K_{IS}.
\]

\end_inset

\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Promote the dominant occupied and empty representatives, as in the current
 enrichment procedure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This replaces the full active-to-Slater enrichment matrix
\begin_inset Formula $K_{AS}$
\end_inset

 by the smaller impurity-to-Slater matrix
\begin_inset Formula $K_{IS}$
\end_inset

.
 The active bath orbitals do not directly request new representatives.
 Instead, the Slater sector is kept in a bath eigenbasis, and new active
 directions enter only through their direct hybridization with the impurity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Energy-weighted variant
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A stronger version is to include the diagonal bath energies as a simple
 imaginary-time or resolvent dressing.
 In the diagonal bath basis, the useful Krylov directions generated through
 the bath are of the form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
K_{IS},\quad K_{IS}K_{SS},\quad K_{IS}K_{SS}^{2},\ldots
\]

\end_inset

or, from a ground-state correction viewpoint, roughly
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{K_{I k}}{E-\epsilon_k}.
\]

\end_inset

Thus one can enrich from a dressed impurity coupling
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W_{I k}=K_{I k}\,f(\epsilon_k,n_k),
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $n_k$
\end_inset

 is the Slater occupation.
 Simple first choices for 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 are
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(\epsilon_k,n_k)=1,\qquad
f(\epsilon_k,n_k)=\frac{1}{|\epsilon_k-\mu|+\eta},
\]

\end_inset

or separate particle and hole denominators around the Fermi level.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Expected tradeoff
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This approach is approximate, but enrichment itself is already a heuristic
 step.
 The main benefit is that most GS iterations could use impurity-only enrichment,
 which is much cheaper than repeatedly coupling the entire active window to
 the full Slater sector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The main risk is missing screening or cloud orbitals that couple mostly through
 already-active bath orbitals rather than directly through the impurity.
 A practical compromise is to do cheap impurity-only enrichment on most iterations
 and occasionally perform the full active-to-Slater enrichment as a refresh.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Conclusion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The idea should not be described as applying the real-time bath interaction
 picture literally to the GS problem.
 A better description is: diagonalize the Slater bath so bath-only propagation
 is trivial, then use impurity-bath hybridization, optionally dressed by simple
 energy denominators, to choose representative Slater orbitals for enrichment.
\end_layout

